# Frontend configuration. Copy to `.env.local` and fill in after the contracts + subgraph are deployed.
#
# ⚠️ READ THIS FIRST. A `VITE_` prefix is PUBLICATION, not configuration.
# Vite inlines every `VITE_*` value into the emitted JavaScript, so anything with that prefix is
# plaintext in `dist` and readable by every visitor.
# A credential therefore goes in a variable WITHOUT the prefix (`RPC_UPSTREAM_URL` below), read only
# by `vite.config.ts` in the Node process that runs the build and the dev server.
# This file said so in writing from build #32 onwards and the deployed config did it anyway, which is
# why `npm run build` now FAILS on a credential-shaped URL in the output rather than warning about
# one - see `build/bundleCredentialGuard.ts` and `docs/security-checklist.md#rpc-key-protection`.

# Chain to target: 4663 (Robinhood mainnet) or 46630 (Robinhood testnet). Defaults to testnet.
VITE_CHAIN_ID=46630


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RPC: the keyed endpoint (server-side only)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# RPC_UPSTREAM_URL is the keyed endpoint, and it is deliberately NOT prefixed.
# Whatever serves `VITE_RPC_PROXY_PATH` reads it and forwards upstream with the key attached, so the
# browser only ever learns an origin it was already talking to.
#
# ⚠️ WHAT THIS DOES NOT BUY, stated plainly because it is easy to over-claim: a proxy is not a
# boundary against USE, only against THEFT. It is open to anyone who can reach the site, so the
# exposure moves from "the key can be extracted and spent anywhere" to "our origin can be used as an
# RPC". Only the second of those is fixable after the fact, which is what makes it the better
# position rather than a solved problem.
#
# ONE Alchemy key serves both chains; only the subdomain differs. MATCH IT TO VITE_CHAIN_ID:
#   46630 (testnet) -> https://robinhood-testnet.g.alchemy.com/v2/<your-key>
#   4663  (mainnet) -> https://robinhood-mainnet.g.alchemy.com/v2/<your-key>
#
# Robinhood's own docs recommend Alchemy; QuickNode, Blockdaemon, dRPC and Validation Cloud also
# serve 4663. All of them require an account, and measured 2026-08-01 there is no usable keyless
# endpoint for these chains beyond the public one (docs/rpc-capability.md).
#
# SETTLED in build #32 - the frontend runs on Alchemy, and the indexer deliberately does NOT.
# The two endpoints are complementary rather than interchangeable: Alchemy is a genuine archive node
# (consistent eth_call 20M blocks deep, where the public endpoint prunes at ~5,000) but its free
# tier caps eth_getLogs to a 10-BLOCK range, and graph-node scans in 1000-block ranges.
# So the browser takes Alchemy and the indexer keeps the public endpoint.
# Do NOT "simplify" by pointing both at one provider.
RPC_UPSTREAM_URL=

# VITE_RPC_PROXY_PATH is the same-origin path the app sends RPC to. `/rpc` is the documented value.
# The app has to be TOLD a proxy exists rather than assuming one: on a host with no such route the
# request returns an HTML 404, which is a successful HTTP response carrying a body viem cannot parse,
# so every read in the app would pay for that round trip. See `src/lib/rpcEndpoints.ts`.
#
# ⚠️ WHO SERVES THE PATH DIFFERS BY ENVIRONMENT, and so does how you configure it:
#   dev / preview - `vite.config.ts` serves it, and ONLY in those two commands. It mounts the proxy
#                   only when RPC_UPSTREAM_URL is set too, so here you set BOTH or NEITHER.
#   production    - the HOST serves it (a rewrite, an edge function or a reverse proxy). Set
#                   VITE_RPC_PROXY_PATH in the BUILD environment, because it is inlined at build
#                   time, and give RPC_UPSTREAM_URL to whatever answers the path at RUNTIME. In this
#                   case RPC_UPSTREAM_URL is deliberately absent from the build.
#
# ⚠️ Otherwise LEAVE THIS BLANK, because the app trusts it: a configured path becomes the FIRST
# endpoint in the list whether or not anything answers it.
#
# An ABSOLUTE URL is also accepted, which is what lets the proxy live on a separate origin. ⚠️ That
# case is no longer same-origin, so the proxy must send CORS headers this app's origin satisfies.
VITE_RPC_PROXY_PATH=

# ALLOW_BUNDLED_RPC_CREDENTIAL=1 is the OPT-OUT, and it is the only supported way to ship a key in
# the bundle: it acknowledges that the key is domain-allowlisted at the provider, and downgrades the
# build failure to a warning. It never silences it, because an allowlist is a provider-side control
# this build cannot verify.
# On that route the key goes in VITE_RPC_URL below - published, by definition - and
# VITE_RPC_PROXY_PATH stays blank. RPC_UPSTREAM_URL is no use there, since nothing would serve it.
# ⚠️ A domain allowlist is Referer-based and a non-browser client can set any Referer it likes.
# It reduces casual abuse; it is not authentication. Leave this unset unless it is a decision.
# ALLOW_BUNDLED_RPC_CREDENTIAL=1


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RPC: the public endpoints (inlined into the bundle, so PUBLIC BY CONSTRUCTION)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Endpoints are tried in strict preference order, deduplicated: the proxy, then VITE_RPC_URL, then
# VITE_RPC_URL_2, then the chain's documented public endpoint. viem's `fallback` runs with ranking
# off, so a healthy first entry serves every request and the rest exist only for failure.

# VITE_RPC_URL is the preferred DIRECT endpoint.
# ⚠️ Do not put a secret here. The build refuses to emit one unless ALLOW_BUNDLED_RPC_CREDENTIAL=1
# is set, which is the deliberate opt-out described above and the only case where a key belongs in
# this variable.
# Leave the public endpoint out of this list - `rpcEndpoints.ts` appends it automatically as the last
# resort, so naming it here only creates a duplicate.
VITE_RPC_URL=

# VITE_RPC_URL_2 is an independent SECOND provider, used only when the one before it fails. This is
# what makes the fallback real redundancy rather than a list of one - see `src/lib/wagmi.ts`.
# It matters more than it looks: the official endpoint is a pool of nodes with differing history
# retention, so a historical read can miss on one provider and succeed on another
# (`docs/rpc-capability.md`), and that error is not one viem retries.
#
# NOTE this URL ships in the browser bundle, exactly like VITE_RPC_URL, under the same rule.
VITE_RPC_URL_2=

# ⚠️ The WALLET is a separate audience and is never offered any of the above.
# `chain.rpcUrls.default` carries the public endpoint only, because wagmi hands its first entry to
# `wallet_addEthereumChain` and MetaMask STORES what it receives as the user's own endpoint for the
# network - so a key there bills us for every visitor's wallet traffic, invisibly, and survives
# rotating the bundle. See `src/config/chain.ts`; four tests fail if the two lists are ever unified.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Contract addresses
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Deployed launchpad contract addresses (from contracts/ deploy - see docs/deploy.md).
# While these are the zero address the app runs in "not configured" mode with a banner.
VITE_FACTORY_ADDRESS=0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000000
VITE_GRADUATION_MANAGER_ADDRESS=0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000000

# The platform's own Uniswap V3 SwapRouter (from the same deploy) - enables the swap page for
# graduated TOKEN/WETH pools. While zero, the swap page shows a "not configured" notice.
VITE_SWAP_ROUTER_ADDRESS=0x0000000000000000000000000000000000000000

# The platform's own QuoterV2 (contracts/script/DeployQuoter.s.sol). OPTIONAL - leave unset and the
# swap page falls back to a labelled slot0 spot-price estimate that ignores fee and price impact.
VITE_QUOTER_ADDRESS=

# Canonical WETH9. Optional - src/config/chain.ts picks the right one per VITE_CHAIN_ID (mainnet and
# testnet WETH9 live at DIFFERENT addresses). Only set this for a bespoke deployment.
VITE_WETH9_ADDRESS=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Indexer
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# GraphQL endpoint of the self-hosted graph-node subgraph (see subgraph/README.md). The subgraph is
# deployed under the name `octopus/octopus`, so BOTH path segments are required. The compose
# stack in subgraph/docker publishes graph-node's port 8000 on host port 8100.
VITE_SUBGRAPH_URL=http://localhost:8100/subgraphs/name/octopus/octopus
